<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  HugMesh shared SpotBugs INCLUDE filter.

  CURATED, not kitchen-sink. SpotBugs ships hundreds of bug patterns; an INCLUDE
  filter means ONLY the patterns named here are ever reported — anything not
  listed is invisible, no matter what the detectors find. Adding a pattern here is
  how a check gets turned ON.

  It covers two things:

  (1) The org's own convention items D/E (representation exposure + mutable static
      state), owned by SpotBugs core.
  (2) The FindSecBugs security patterns.

  ============================================================================
  NOTE ON FINDSECBUGS PATTERN NAMES — the previous version of this comment was
  WRONG in a way that silently disabled all security scanning.
  ============================================================================
  It claimed FindSecBugs "reuses the same EI_EXPOSE_REP* / MS_* pattern names for
  its security-flavoured detectors (keys, tokens, byte[]), so listing the pattern
  names here is enough to surface both". That is FALSE.

  Verified against findsecbugs-plugin 1.14.0's own findbugs.xml: it defines 144
  BugPattern types, and NOT ONE of them is an EI_EXPOSE_REP*/MS_* name — the
  overlap with the D/E list below is exactly zero. FindSecBugs uses its own
  namespace (SQL_INJECTION, HARD_CODE_KEY, PATH_TRAVERSAL_IN, ...). Because this
  is an INCLUDE filter, naming only the 9 D/E patterns suppressed all 144
  security patterns: FindSecBugs was on the classpath and running, but none of its
  findings could ever be reported. Security scanning had effectively never run.

  The 137 FindSecBugs patterns admitted below are the ones measured at ZERO
  findings across the whole fleet (effort=Max, threshold=Low, real exclude
  baseline applied, 1311 classes, 0 missing classes / 0 analysis errors), so
  admitting them costs no new build failures today while making the gate catch
  these categories on all FUTURE code.

  DELIBERATELY NOT ADMITTED — the 7 patterns that currently fire. Admitting any
  of them would red-line the fleet immediately (hugmesh-parent runs
  spotbugs:check with failOnError=true). Each needs its own triage/campaign:
    SPRING_ENDPOINT (411)      informational "this is an endpoint"; noise in a
                               fail gate — should never be admitted.
    CRLF_INJECTION_LOGS (599)  real pattern (log forging), but a fleet-wide
                               remediation campaign, not a gate flip.
    IMPROPER_UNICODE (71)      high false-positive rate.
    REDOS, UNSAFE_HASH_EQUALS, SPRING_CSRF_UNRESTRICTED_REQUEST_MAPPING,
    COMMAND_INJECTION (9 total) triaged against the real code and found to be
                               false positives (argv-form ProcessBuilder with no
                               shell; comparison of PUBLIC SSH host-key
                               fingerprints; JWT-bearer chains unreachable by an
                               ambient cookie; bounded {0,61} hostname regexes).
                               Admit later behind NARROW PER-SITE exclude entries
                               — never as a blanket pattern suppression.

  ROLLOUT / FAIL MODE: hugmesh-parent runs spotbugs:check with
  <failOnError>true</failOnError>. On the check goal `failOnError` is the ONLY
  fail switch and it gates BOTH real findings AND SpotBugs-internal analysis
  errors together. `failOnViolation` is NOT a parameter of this goal (it belongs
  to maven-pmd-plugin / maven-checkstyle-plugin); passing it is silently ignored
  apart from a "[WARNING] Parameter 'failOnViolation' is unknown" line — which is
  exactly how this gate sat fail-OPEN until hugmesh-parent PR #15. Do not
  re-add it. The known-pattern baseline lives in spotbugs-exclude.xml.

  Note that spotbugs-exclude.xml's *Test / *Tests / *IT / generated-sources
  matches carry no <Bug> element, so they are unscoped and drop ALL patterns —
  including the 144 FindSecBugs ones — in those classes. That is intended, but it
  makes that file load-bearing for the security coverage admitted here.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

  <!-- ===================== D/E conventions (SpotBugs core) ================= -->

  <!-- D. Representation exposure on instance state. -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
  </Match>
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
  </Match>
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="MS_EXPOSE_REP"/>
  </Match>

  <!-- D. Mutable static fields exposed / shared (arrays, collections). -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="MS_MUTABLE_ARRAY"/>
  </Match>
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="MS_MUTABLE_COLLECTION"/>
  </Match>
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="MS_MUTABLE_COLLECTION_PKGPROTECT"/>
  </Match>
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="MS_PKGPROTECT"/>
  </Match>

  <!-- E. Mutable static state that should be final. -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="MS_SHOULD_BE_FINAL"/>
  </Match>
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="MS_CANNOT_BE_FINAL"/>
  </Match>

  <!-- ================ FindSecBugs 1.14.0 — 137 zero-finding ================ -->

  <!-- Injection: SQL / LDAP / XPath / expression-language / template / OS-command. -->
  <Match><Bug pattern="AWS_QUERY_INJECTION"/></Match>
  <Match><Bug pattern="BEAN_PROPERTY_INJECTION"/></Match>
  <Match><Bug pattern="CUSTOM_INJECTION"/></Match>
  <Match><Bug pattern="EL_INJECTION"/></Match>
  <Match><Bug pattern="EXTERNAL_CONFIG_CONTROL"/></Match>
  <Match><Bug pattern="FORMAT_STRING_MANIPULATION"/></Match>
